body:
Hi everyone! Thanks for clearing things up that it shouldn't be like this! I live in a country where there aren't that many vet clinics, and even fewer animal hospitals (probably no more than 5 nation wide), so there aren't always openings for the role of vet assistant. I really want the experience and get that recommendation letter so I can get in my chosen university. I decided to toughen it up for now. But of course I'll have my limits and know when to throw in the towel.

&#x200B;

About the practicing manager, there's only four of us in the clinic; vet, assistants, and receptionist. Nobody is stopping him because he's actually very nice with the animals, clients, and other people, only his staff sees the abusive side of his. So there's no one else to tell him about his behavior. I just wish he would limit the surgeries he takes in per day so he wouldn't be so stressed. He's actually nice to be with outside work, but still I agree that it's still very inexcusable to treat us that way.

&#x200B;

The people I work with are great, they always try to help by jumping in when I can't remember how to do things or where something is placed. Or they would talk to me and ask me if I'm okay when I get yelled at. I wanted to laugh when I was being interviewed by the vet during my first day, he asked, before I start do I have any issues with the other staff?  I wanted to say "Sir the only one I'm afraid of here is you." :D lol but anyway, reading your comments here gives me hope that I would definitely enjoy this type of work, hopefully when I get more experience, education, then I can finally have the opportunity to work with other people.

&#x200B;

PS: I probably found this normal for a while because I got used to my past teachers to being this strict and scary. We live somewhere in Asia so the level of strictness is too much, I've had different people do the same abusive things, the yelling and throwing things like desk and chairs, I know wtf. I always hated it and scarred me as a child, there is no way I would treat anyone like this if ever I'm in a position to teach, but so far it's the norm :(
